# Folder rename Aug 17 2026. Trends: "furniture repair" (26) is 2.6x
# "furniture restoration" (10); "custom cabinets" (21) is ~4x "built in
# cabinets" (~5). 301s so the old URLs keep whatever they earned.
/furniture-restoration/*   /furniture-repair/:splat   301
/furniture-restoration/    /furniture-repair/         301
/cabinets-and-built-ins/*  /custom-cabinets/:splat    301
/cabinets-and-built-ins/   /custom-cabinets/          301

# Netlify Pretty URLs serves every city page at both /city-tx and
# /city-tx.html as a 200, so each page was indexed twice and splitting its
# own impressions. Canonicals, the sitemap and all internal links already use
# .html, so the extensionless form 301s to it. These are exact paths, one per
# page, rather than a splat: /custom-signs/* would also match
# /custom-signs/tyler-tx.html and send it to .html.html, and would match
# /custom-signs/ itself.
#
# Forced (301!) on purpose. Pretty URLs makes /city-tx resolve to a real
# asset, and an unforced rule loses to an existing asset, so the plain 301
# never fired: the extensionless URL was still a 200 on the deploy preview.
# Forcing evaluates the rule ahead of the asset. Safe here because no rule's
# source contains .html, so forcing cannot catch the .html URL it redirects to.
/custom-cabinets/arp-tx           /custom-cabinets/arp-tx.html           301!
/custom-cabinets/big-sandy-tx     /custom-cabinets/big-sandy-tx.html     301!
